Illustrative visual grammars, not universal emotional associations.

Color, typography, spacing, and hierarchy function like grammar. They tell the audience how to assemble meaning.

And the rules differ between cultures, genres, audiences, and historical periods.

2. The visual dialects of different cultures

A particularly good way to think about cultural art is that it has dialects, registers, and historical lineages, just as verbal languages do.

Akan / Asante visual proverbs — Ghana

Adinkra textiles use symbols that can invoke proverbs, social values, history, and ancestral relationships. A visual form can stand for an entire lesson.

The Dwennimmen ram’s-horn motif, for example, is associated with strength and humility.

The Smithsonian describes these textiles as communicating culturally specific messages.

Japanese aesthetics — Ma and yohaku

Certain Japanese artistic traditions give deliberate importance to intervals, unoccupied space, and the relationships between visible elements.

Ma can concern meaningful spatial or temporal intervals; yohaku is associated with blank or unoccupied space in composition.

Emptiness becomes active through what surrounds it.

Islamic artistic traditions — Geometry and calligraphy

Geometric repetition, vegetal ornament, and calligraphy can connect mathematics, visual rhythm, architecture, and written meaning.

A written word may simultaneously function as a linguistic message and a visual composition. Styles vary substantially across centuries and regions, including Iran, North Africa, Spain, and South Asia.

European heraldry — Identity through composition

Shields, heraldic colors, charges, and their arrangements operate under formal conventions.

A lion, for instance, is not just an illustration of an animal. In a coat of arms, it can help identify an individual, lineage, institution, or political entity.

The visual symbol acts as a persistent identifier across objects, flags, seals, and buildings.

And there is a subtle historical pattern here: these systems aren’t isolated.

They borrow from neighbors. Trade routes carry patterns. Religions transform imagery. New technologies alter what artists can produce. Conquest can displace cultural practices; migration can preserve and recombine them.

The visual language of a tradition is itself a historical artifact.

That means we could build a History Stereoscope of visual forms, connecting how a pattern appeared, traveled, changed, and acquired new meanings.

Culture can also change where your eyes go

A 2025 eye-tracking study compared Japanese and American readers viewing manga and American comics.

For some page types, American participants attended relatively more to foreground objects, while Japanese participants attended relatively more to backgrounds. But when the artistic perspective changed, those patterns also changed. Reading experience and individual cognitive styles mattered.

That’s a powerful observation.

Visual culture doesn’t merely provide symbols for interpretation. It can help train patterns of attention—and artists can deliberately redirect those patterns.

But these are group-level, task-dependent findings, not fixed properties of Japanese or American people.

Even color meanings are historically contingent

Consider red.

A red wedding garment in one tradition might reinforce associations with prosperity or celebration. In a different setting, red might foreground danger, urgency, passion, or political identity.

A 2026 experimental study found implicit associations between red and happiness or weddings among its sample of Chinese university students. It did not find the same implicit association for every culturally prominent concept involving red.

So a color can have several culturally learned meanings simultaneously, with context determining which becomes salient.

This is why translating a design requires more than copying its palette.

3. How to connect different visual languages without homogenizing them

I’d build a Cultural Visual Rosetta Engine.

Instead of matching surface symbols, it maps the function and relationships underlying them.

Say we want to communicate protection of a community.

One design might use a shield. Another might emphasize a protective enclosure. Another might show a group collectively sheltering a vulnerable member.

These images are not culturally interchangeable, and they may carry very different histories. But we can compare their mechanisms.

Example: translating the idea of protection

Defensive object

Protected boundary

Collective solidarity

Different visual structures, overlapping intended relationships.

The engineering sequence would be:

Source artwork
  ↓
Identify objects, layout, symbolism and context
  ↓
Recover literal and implied relationships
  ↓
Separate culturally specific meanings
from more transferable mechanisms
  ↓
Find target-culture visual possibilities
  ↓
Recompose using target-medium conventions
  ↓
Compare emotional, semantic and visual effects
  ↓
Validate with knowledgeable interpreters
  ↓
Preserve both versions and their differences

The result isn’t a universal symbol dictionary where every object has one fixed meaning.

It’s a network of situated interpretations, with provenance, confidence, and alternative readings.

This also connects directly to your Cultural Sovereignty Membrane: communities should be able to decide which knowledge is public, what can be adapted, and what should remain within its original cultural setting.

Interoperability without erasure.

4. Other languages beyond the visual

Once you recognize how visual grammar operates, you start finding equivalent structures everywhere.

Expressive system	Its vocabulary	Its grammar
Dance	Poses and movements	Timing, transitions, spatial relationships
Architecture	Materials, openings, walls	Circulation, enclosure, proportion, sequence
Clothing	Fabric, shape, color	Layering, combinations, appropriateness to occasion
Film	Shots, sounds, frames	Editing, continuity, pacing, juxtaposition
Games	Actions, rewards, constraints	Rules, state transitions, consequences
Music	Notes, timbres, motifs	Rhythm, harmony, repetition, transformation
Gesture	Hand forms, movement, orientation	Culturally learned gesture patterns; signed languages have full linguistic grammars
Ritual	Objects, gestures, utterances	Sequence, roles, participation, repetition

They aren’t all literally languages in the same technical linguistic sense. Some are full natural languages; others are semiotic systems, artistic grammars, or useful analogies.

But they can all carry structured meaning.

And they can interact.

A film score can change the emotional interpretation of a scene. Architecture can modify how a ritual unfolds. A costume can convey social status before a character speaks. A game can make you experience scarcity rather than merely tell you scarcity exists.

This leads to a distinction I’d preserve in the Pantheon:

Descriptive meaning: telling an observer about a state of affairs.

Enactive meaning: creating conditions in which the observer experiences or performs an analogous state.

A movie can describe fear. A game can make you manage uncertainty with incomplete information. Different interfaces, related meanings.

5. The less appreciated transformations AI is bringing

I think some of AI’s most consequential changes are not simply that it performs existing jobs faster.

They’re that it changes the cost and feasibility of whole categories of thought, discovery, coordination, and creation.

Several are already observable. Others are emerging possibilities, and their success will depend on reliability, institutions, and human choices.

Ten transformations worth watching

1. Inverse design: starting from the desired effect

Traditional design often moves from components to an outcome.

AI-assisted inverse design starts with the properties you want and searches for structures that might produce them.

This isn’t just a packaging or art phenomenon. In 2025, researchers published MatterGen, a generative model for proposing inorganic materials under desired property constraints. Physical validation remains necessary.

The abstraction is: describe the destination, then search backward for possible paths.

2. Representational arbitrage

A difficult problem in one representation may become easier in another.

Convert a complicated process into a diagram. Convert the diagram into a state machine. Analyze the state machine, then translate the result into operational instructions.

AI can help discover and traverse these representational pathways.

The gain comes from choosing a useful mathematical or expressive structure—not simply changing the appearance of the problem.

3. Scientific hypothesis generation becomes a broader search

AI systems can generate competing hypotheses, identify relevant literature, and propose experiments.

A 2026 Nature paper on Co-Scientist reported empirical validation of AI-generated hypotheses across biomedical research problems. That represents progress in hypothesis generation and evaluation—not the elimination of laboratory science.

Science becomes increasingly a process of navigating, testing, and pruning large spaces of possibilities.

4. Expensive simulations gain fast approximate counterparts

AI can learn useful approximations of complex systems from data.

ECMWF put its AI weather forecasting system into operations in February 2025 alongside traditional numerical forecasting. It reported roughly 1,000-fold lower energy use per forecast, with improvements on several prediction metrics.

Faster models can make it practical to investigate more scenarios, although they still have resolution, distribution-shift, and physical-fidelity limitations.

This is particularly relevant to your idea of rapidly testing multiple plausible futures.

5. Tacit expertise becomes easier to externalize

Much expertise lives in judgments that people struggle to articulate.

A craftsperson may sense that something is wrong without immediately knowing how to explain it.

With AI, a person can compare examples, annotate failures, demonstrate corrections, and progressively extract decision rules.

Some of this could become reusable instruction, training material, evaluators, or assisted workflows.

Capturing a skill’s observable patterns, however, is not the same as capturing the expert’s entire embodied competence.

6. Work products can be prestiged into higher-level tools

This is the game-mechanics analogy I think you’re getting at.

Solve a problem once. Preserve the useful method. Turn that method into an operator. Use the operator to solve a whole class of problems. Then build operators that compose the earlier operators.

You’re not merely accumulating outputs. You’re accumulating the capacity to produce new outputs.

That is a recursive advantage when the abstractions are genuinely reusable.

7. Attention itself becomes a more programmable interface

An AI system can potentially reorganize information by urgency, uncertainty, spatial relevance, expertise, or emotional salience.

Imagine a research interface that shows broad patterns from afar, exceptions at medium range, and original evidence at close range.

Instead of forcing the user to search a fixed document hierarchy, the representation adapts to their task.

That connects directly to your Semantic Zoom and Multiscale Menagerie.

8. Cross-cultural collaboration can gain new interfaces

AI can help reconstruct references, compare cultural concepts, reveal translation alternatives, and preserve ambiguity rather than forcing a single equivalent.

That opens opportunities for cultural preservation and collaboration.

But it also creates risks of misrepresentation, unauthorized extraction, and flattening distinct traditions. Research involving Indigenous-language machine translation emphasizes meaningful participation from language communities.

9. Evaluation becomes more important, and sometimes more expensive

If producing a candidate is easy, distinguishing good candidates becomes a central bottleneck.

A 2025 randomized study of experienced open-source developers found that access to contemporary AI coding tools increased task-completion time by 19% in its tested setting. Verification and correction were among the challenges. The result should not be generalized to all developers or tools.

Fast generation does not guarantee fast, dependable completion.

This is why independent testing, provenance, and failure detection become strategic capabilities.

10. A new aesthetic search space opens up

Artists can explore compositions, narrative structures, and cross-medium combinations that would previously have been prohibitively expensive to prototype.

That could create new genres, not just more examples of existing genres.

But the opposite pressure exists too: systems trained on common patterns can reproduce familiar aesthetics excessively, leading to stylistic convergence.

The difference is whether AI is used to repeat likely patterns or deliberately explore and test less familiar ones.

6. Prestiging is especially important

Let’s formalize your gaming analogy.

In a conventional production system:

\text{Labor}\rightarrow\text{Artifact}

In a recursive learning system:

\text{Labor}\rightarrow\text{Artifact}
\rightarrow\text{Reusable method}
\rightarrow\text{Higher-level capability}

And then:

C_{n+1}=C_n+\Delta C_{\text{validated}}

Where is the system’s accumulated useful capability, and the addition is a validated improvement—not merely another generated document.

The Prestige Ladder

Level 1

Create an artifact

Level 2

Extract its method

Level 3

Generalize into a reusable operator

Level 4

Compose multiple operators

Level 5

Build a system that discovers better operators

Take the Wild Hawg boar design.

At the first level, AI produces an image.

At the second, you articulate why some designs work better: tusk anatomy, contour flow, visual hierarchy, color harmony, and realism constraints.

At the third, you formalize those observations into reusable design rules.

At the fourth, those rules join your brand system, marketing system, audience testing, and production constraints.

At the fifth, a system could compare new concepts, detect violations, learn from feedback, and propose improved alternatives.

The resulting asset isn’t just the design.

It’s the capability to make families of increasingly consistent designs, potentially with less repeated effort.

That’s the prestige mechanic.

The catch is that without meaningful validation, you can also prestige errors. A flawed heuristic becomes a reusable generator of flawed results. Recursive development therefore needs pruning as much as accumulation.

7. The next bottleneck: choosing what deserves to exist

Here’s another change I suspect will become increasingly important.

Historically, there were many situations where only a few possible designs, explanations, or hypotheses could be explored because each attempt was expensive.

As exploration gets cheaper, the scarcity moves.

Older bottlenecks

Production capacity, specialist labor, manual iteration, access to tools.

Emerging bottlenecks

Trustworthy evaluation, distinctive judgment, attention, physical validation, shared purpose.

More possible outputs ≠ more valuable outcomes

This also changes education.

Rather than simply training someone to reproduce an answer, we can increasingly train them to interrogate its assumptions, select the right representation, test alternatives, and determine whether the result survives contact with reality.

It changes organizations too. A small team may be able to explore more possibilities, while simultaneously struggling to govern the flood of generated material.

And it changes creativity: personal taste becomes more consequential because an individual can exercise it across a larger design space.

8. The Pantheon opportunity: a Universal Grammar Observatory

I would join these discoveries under a new architectural layer rather than build isolated modules for every medium.

The Universal Grammar Observatory would study the structures through which different systems encode, transform, and transmit information.

It would have five connected capabilities:

Capability	Purpose
Grammar Atlas	Catalogue conventions across languages, cultures, arts, organisms, and computational systems
Cultural Stereoscope	Trace the historical evolution and exchange of meanings
Transduction Engine	Find and test meaningful cross-medium correspondences
Aesthetic Forensics	Infer what formal mechanisms produce an observed artistic effect
Prestige Engine	Convert validated discoveries into reusable operators and higher-level systems

The central cycle becomes:

\boxed{
\text{Observe}
\rightarrow
\text{Decode}
\rightarrow
\text{Compare}
\rightarrow
\text{Translate}
\rightarrow
\text{Test}
\rightarrow
\text{Prestige}
}

And then recurse.

The key is keeping distinct types of correspondence separate.

An analogy can be beautiful but physically wrong. A translation can be linguistically accurate but emotionally misleading. A cultural comparison can be structurally useful while historically unsupported.

The system should explicitly record which properties are preserved, which are altered, and which are only conjectured.

One more possibility: discover grammars that humans haven’t named yet

This may be the most interesting frontier.

Human beings have spent millennia discovering recurring structures in nature, art, language, and mathematics.

We name them: symmetry, alliteration, recursion, counterpoint, perspective, feedback, resonance, metaphor.

But those named structures are not necessarily the complete set of useful patterns.

AI may help find recurring organizations of information that span different domains but have not previously been recognized as belonging to the same family.

A visual composition, a musical phrase, a social convention, and a biological signaling system might share a measurable pattern of expectation and resolution.

The discovery wouldn’t establish that they are literally the same phenomenon.

It could, however, reveal a transferable principle worth testing.

That’s where the Grammar Observatory would become something more than a catalogue.

It would become an instrument for comparative discovery.

And that connects directly to your earlier insight about seeing the same gem through different faces:

The ideal system doesn’t force every face to look identical. It learns what remains invariant as the gem turns, what changes with the viewing angle, and what only becomes visible when the light hits it differently.

AI’s deeper promise is not merely that more things can be created. It’s that more transformations between ways of understanding, creating, and testing things may become accessible.

The Pantheon’s job would be to make those transformations explicit, composable, reversible where possible, and grounded enough that the next discovery can genuinely build on the last.